There are two license types. The receipt says which one the Licensee bought.

1. Standard License

   The Licensee may:

   a. Run the Software, modified or not, as a feedback tool for the Licensee's own company and
      products, with any number of workspaces, users and servers.
   b. Set up and run the Software for the Licensee's clients, for each client's own use, as a
      freelancer or agency. Each client may use it for their own company and products.
   c. Modify the Software in any way and rebrand it (name, logo, colors, domains).
   d. Let employees and contractors work on the Software for these purposes.

   The Licensee may not offer the Software, or a product built from it, as a paid or free
   hosted service that other companies sign up to and use for their own feedback (a "Hosted
   Service"). That requires the Extended License.

2. Extended License

   Everything in the Standard License, and the Licensee may also run the Software, modified and
   rebranded, as a Hosted Service: their own feedback, roadmap and changelog product that any
   number of other companies sign up to and pay for, under the Licensee's own brand.
   One Extended License covers one Hosted Service brand.

3. What neither license allows

   a. Selling, sublicensing, sharing, giving away or publishing the Software's source code, or
      any substantial part of it, on its own or as a template, starter kit, boilerplate, script
      or code generator, whether modified or not. Customers of a Hosted Service use it; they
      don't receive its source code.
   b. Making the source code of the Software publicly available, for example in a public code
      repository.
   c. Using the Software to build a product that competes with the Software itself, such as
      another self-hosted feedback script for sale.
   d. Removing this license or the copyright notice from the Software's files.

